WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:06.600
I talked a little bit last time about what
we were going to cover but we didn’t get

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.120
quite through it so I’m going to finish
that. For those of you who weren’t here

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:16.250
last time you can watch the lecture because
it’s online. So, we stopped at the depth

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:22.159
psychologists, really, and those are the psychoanalysts.
And psychoanalysts are people who are concerned

00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:29.890
with—well, for a psychoanalyst, roughly speaking,
you’re a collection of loosely integrated

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:35.100
spirits. That’s one way of thinking about
it—or sub personalities. You’re not a

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.100
unity. And those subpersonalities have their
basis in, well in all sorts of things. They

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:46.370
might have their basis in past especially
traumatic experience or in patterns of socialization

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:51.440
that characterized your family, but they might
also have their basis in fundamental biological

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:58.540
motivational systems. So, for example, a psychoanalyst
would contextualize anger, or perhaps sexual

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:04.390
attraction, as a subpersonality. And the reason
for that is that—well you know what it’s

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:09.760
like when you get angry. You get angry at
someone you love and you know, first of all,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.630
you know, and it’s some level you’re wishing
harm. Now, that might only be that you want

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.930
to win the argument and you want them to be
nicely crushed while you do it, but so there’s

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.950
a desire that comes along with the anger.
But there’s a lot of other things that happen

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.430
too. So, for example, if someone’s annoying
you, even if you love them, the probability

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.130
that at that moment you’re going to be able
to easily access all the memories you have

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:37.440
about how annoying they are and have difficult
time accessing the memories about how wonderful

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.120
they’ve been to you is quite high. You know
and you know that because sometimes you get

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.520
angry with someone and you have an argument
with them and you sort of clue in later, you

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:50.030
snap out of your more or less possessed state
and you think, “Yeah, well you know, I really

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:55.200
wasn’t taking the context into mind and
I was kind of harsh.” So, well so and so

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.510
then you think, “Well, who’s in control.”
And that’s the question the psychoanalysts

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:04.280
are really interested in and their answer
is it’s not generally you.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.229
And so it’s very terrifying reading in some
sense because, you know, people like to think

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:14.940
of themselves as masters of their own house
so to speak and it’s just, it’s only vaguely

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:22.450
true. And, you know, people—you know that
too because you make maybe New Year’s Eve—ah,

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.830
what do you call those?—resolutions. How
you’re going to be a better person. I think

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.640
the most common ones is man, you’re going
to hit the gym three times a week. It’s

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.599
like, “No, you’re not actually!” And
so very few people do and that’s partly

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:37.849
because, well it’s hard and so you don’t
want to do hard things. And the reason for

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:41.819
that is because they’re hard, so it doesn’t
take much explanation. But it’s also because

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:46.230
you can’t just tell yourself what to do.
And that’s annoying too because life would

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.660
be a lot easier if you could just say, “Okay,
well you know, sit down, study for two hours,

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:54.750
don’t watch cats on YouTube—or whatever
it is that you’re watching.” And no, no,

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.849
no, that isn’t what happens. You sit there
and you study for a while and then, you know,

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:01.569
you’re thinking about cats and the next
thing you know you’re watching haunted mansions

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:09.629
in London or some damn thing. You know, so
and it’s a scary idea in some sense because

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:13.110
if you think well, if you’re not really
master of your own house, you know if you

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:17.280
don’t know yourself under full voluntary
control—and you certainly don’t—then

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.770
what does?

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:23.590
And you know Freud is interesting in that
regard because he really talked about the

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:28.030
motive powerful of fundamental motivational
systems. Mostly for Freud it was aggression

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:31.660
and sexuality. And you know those are—those
are big motivators; let’s make no mistake

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:38.180
about it. And so he thought of you as—that
the conscious part of your personality maybe

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:43.940
as the—it’s like the caption of a ship
with a very unruly crew. It’s a nice metaphor

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.900
because of course the ship’s on the ocean
and the oceans can be very stormy. And so

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.370
you can only sort of plot your course and
you have to work with what’s thrown at you.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:57.650
And then the unruly crew is—well, it’s
all your drives. Drives is the wrong way of

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:04.480
thinking about it. I like the psychoanalytic
notion of subpersonality because a subpersonality

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.629
has a viewpoint, there’s things it wants,
and it plans. So it’s a living entity; it’s

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:15.819
not some dead cognitive system, you know.
So Freud was interested in some sense more

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:21.400
about, I would say, the biological underpinnings
of the subpersonalities, you know. Because

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.820
he was quite biologically oriented and he
realized that sexuality and aggression were

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.570
very fundamental subpersonalities.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.880
And then there’s Jung and Jung is much more
terrifying than Freud, which is really saying

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.970
a lot because Freud’s quite terrifying.
But Jung, he’s just terrifying beyond belief.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:44.440
And, you know, for Jung the subpersonalities
that make you up are thousands and thousands

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:48.120
of years old. They’re cultural constructions
that are thousands and thousands of years

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.389
old and they have you in their grip. So, for
Jung, for example, one of the things he said

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:57.840
and I don’t remember where it was—I love
this phrasing, it’s so true. And you can

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.070
think about this for like five years and you
can still think about it after that. One of

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:08.560
the things that Jung said was that people
don’t have ideas; ideas have people. And

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.850
that’s really something to think about because,
you know, just think about your own situation.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:16.889
So no doubt you have political views and you
think, “I have political views.” It’s

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:21.389
like, “Well, no you don’t,” because
you share them with a lot of other people.

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:28.000
So, they’re certainly not yours. And not
only that, they’ve had a very lengthy historical

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.530
development far before you ever showed up.
You know, so they’re rooted—they’re

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:36.710
rooted right at the depths of your culture,
but then of course they’ve been elaborated

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:41.240
by one philosopher or another extensively
over the last two or three hundred years and

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:45.979
there’s this whole system of thought and
poof! It inhabits you brain and then you think,

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:53.220
“Well I’ve got political views.” It’s
like, “No. The political views have you.”

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:58.919
And that’s a very salutary thought because
it’s one of the ways to stop yourself from

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:03.669
being possessed. Because people get possessed
all the time by ideas, right? That’s what

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:07.970
happened in Nazi Germany; that’s what happened
in the Soviet Union. It happens everywhere

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:13.570
all the time. And you might think, “Well,
possessed is a little strong,” but no it’s

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:18.180
not actually. It’s a very nice—it’s
a metaphor obviously, but it’s the right

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:23.690
kind of metaphor because a body of ideas like
political ideas, they’re a living thing,

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:29.610
you know. They’re not some dead, dusty words
on a bookshelf somewhere; they’re an integral

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:35.729
part of a dynamic society. And the motive
force those ideas have motivates you. You’re

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:40.770
in their grip. And so, you can tell when you’re
talking to someone who’s completely possessed

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:45.650
by an idea system or an ideological system
because they’ll just bore you to death unless

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:50.580
you happen to belief exactly the same thing.
So, they’ll spout out a few of their fundamental

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.350
belief axioms and then you can identify which
system they’re part of. And then they just

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:59.770
speak in clichés from that point onward and
you can predict everything they’re going

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:06.310
to say if you know the axioms of the system.
So, it’s very—it’s very non-authentic

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:13.199
speech and that would be a way that say someone
like Rogers or Abraham Maslow, who were humanists

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:17.169
of the 1960’s, might talk about it. Because
the person isn’t speaking from the depths

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:21.310
of their own experience or from even their
embodied knowledge. You know, they’re just

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.940
mouthpieces for abstract ideas.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:29.590
And so it’s very frightening to realize
the extent to which you can be the mouthpiece

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:34.509
for abstract ideas, you know. And it’s actually
one of the dangers of becoming educated, especially

00:07:34.509 --> 00:07:38.389
in a mass system like the university system,
because a lot of what happens when you come

00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:45.880
to university is that you’re fed a set of
predigested ideas—I suppose that’s one

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.199
way of thinking about it, you know. And it’s
very easy for you to think well that those

00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:56.630
are now you’re ideas; they’re not. They’re
not. Lots of ideas you have to earn and if

00:07:56.630 --> 00:08:01.259
it’s a great idea then you really have to
earn it. Because why should you be able to

00:08:01.259 --> 00:08:06.000
come along and like pluck up a great idea?
That’s not how things work. You have to

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.190
earn things that are valuable.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:12.060
So anyways, the depth psychologists, man they’re
so good at that. It’s lovely to read them.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.990
And the problem too is with a lot of people
we’re going to talk about it’s very difficult

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:21.130
to summarize their work. You know, because
often the devil’s in the details. So, you

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:24.590
know, you might look at someone like Piaget
is a good example—the constructivist that

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:28.840
we talked about briefly. Man, the guy wrote
dozens of books; you can’t just summarize

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:34.409
those suckers. It’s not one coherent theory
that you can boil down to, you know, ten axioms

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:38.550
and then you can read it while you’re flying
from Toronto to Montreal and poof you’re

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:43.419
a Piagetian psychologist. Like, it doesn’t
work like that. The people who formulated

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:49.459
these theories were extremely intelligent
and they were often great writers. And so

00:08:49.459 --> 00:08:53.699
a lot of the knowledge is actually in the
writing itself and not in what you can extract

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:58.689
out from the gist, you know. And most of the
theorists: Freud—Jung it’s harder—but

00:08:58.689 --> 00:09:03.329
Freud, Rogers—people like that. You can
extract out the gist of their theories and

00:09:03.329 --> 00:09:11.259
then you can—ah, what would you say?—you
can stereotype it or you can caricature it

00:09:11.259 --> 00:09:15.519
and then you can point out how your caricature
is absurd and you can feel like you’re even

00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:20.550
smarter than Freud. And, you know, you’re
not. So, it’s not a good idea. And so these

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:24.519
people should also be approached with respect
because man they have so much to tell you.

00:09:24.519 --> 00:09:25.709
It’s so useful.

00:09:25.709 --> 00:09:31.119
Okay, so we talked about the depth psychologists,
we talked about the constructivists; we didn’t

00:09:31.119 --> 00:09:35.980
talk about the humanists and the existentialists
and the phenomenologists. It’s a mouthful

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:42.079
of words, you know, so let’s put a little
bit of foundation under those. So, if you’re

00:09:42.079 --> 00:09:48.129
a humanist—to be a humanist means really
to be a creature of the enlightenment. And

00:09:48.129 --> 00:09:52.160
a humanist thought in some sense—at least
this is what the humanists think—they think

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:59.089
of it as post-religious. You know and they
think of humanistic thought as putting the

00:09:59.089 --> 00:10:04.509
value of the human being at the center of
the cosmos in some sense, valuing the person

00:10:04.509 --> 00:10:12.790
as they are, and then also valuing the person
that they could be and promoting the pathways

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:17.929
of development that would allow people to
become what they could be as creatures that

00:10:17.929 --> 00:10:24.949
are valuable in their own right. So, a humanist,
for example, would be very skeptical of claims,

00:10:24.949 --> 00:10:32.449
so let’s say standard Christian claims—and
I use that example because the realm of ideas

00:10:32.449 --> 00:10:38.139
that these thinkers emerged out of was almost
always Judeo-Christian. And, so, you know, the

00:10:38.139 --> 00:10:41.759
roots of their thinking are in those systems
and you can’t understand their thinking

00:10:41.759 --> 00:10:47.410
without understanding that. So, part of humanism
was a rebellion against the idea that life

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:51.369
on earth so to speak was of limited value
because anything of any importance was going

00:10:51.369 --> 00:10:56.249
to happen in the afterlife after you die.
So, it’s like, “No, it’s here and now.

00:10:56.249 --> 00:11:00.720
It’s right now that matters. And you know,
life in the present is what’s important,

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:05.379
and you don’t need some kind of afterlife
or some kind of belief in a transcendent reality

00:11:05.379 --> 00:11:10.019
in order to find value right here and now.”
So you know, fine. There’s a lot of good

00:11:10.019 --> 00:11:11.399
things about that idea.

00:11:11.399 --> 00:11:17.610
You know and the whole idea that you can improve
yourself in some sense is a humanistic idea.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:23.540
And most of you—I mean how many of you believe
that can improve yourself? So, is there anybody

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:29.699
who doesn’t believe that that’s willing
to admit it? So, usually people only don’t

00:11:29.699 --> 00:11:33.499
believe that if they’re very depressed or
hopeless. But it’s not self-evident, you

00:11:33.499 --> 00:11:38.049
know, I mean and in many cultures—you could
think of medieval culture, for example, in

00:11:38.049 --> 00:11:42.379
Europe where you know you were sort of stuck
in your position—there was no real idea

00:11:42.379 --> 00:11:47.319
of self-improvement. You could try not to
sin but you weren’t going to get like radically

00:11:47.319 --> 00:11:53.009
better in some way; you were just who you
were and that was that. But that’s not the

00:11:53.009 --> 00:11:54.209
case with the humanists.

00:11:54.209 --> 00:11:59.879
Now, existentialists, they’re kind of a
different kettle of fish. So, you know, when

00:11:59.879 --> 00:12:04.579
Freud talks about psychopathology—because
a lot of personality is about pathology, right,

00:12:04.579 --> 00:12:09.529
how personalities can go wrong—Freud kind
of thinks of the normal person, roughly speaking,

00:12:09.529 --> 00:12:15.689
as healthy in their normality, but perhaps
transformed into a pathological version of

00:12:15.689 --> 00:12:20.439
themselves by some sort of past trauma. And
that might often be an abusive childhood,

00:12:20.439 --> 00:12:25.459
you know, or maybe a sexually abusive childhood
because, you know, Freud talked a lot about

00:12:25.459 --> 00:12:32.410
that and it happens fairly frequently. So
for Freud, all things considered, if you weren’t

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:36.489
healthy there was a reason for it. You know,
something terrible has happened to you and

00:12:36.489 --> 00:12:40.619
you needed to come to terms with that terrible
thing, however you might do that, and put

00:12:40.619 --> 00:12:45.350
yourself back together and then you could
wander along being a healthy person. But the

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:49.439
existentialists, that’s not how they think
at all. They think the standard human being

00:12:49.439 --> 00:12:55.519
is pathological. And the reason they think
that is because they think that self-conscious

00:12:55.519 --> 00:13:01.410
existence, so life—the life of a creature
who knows it’s bounded in time and space,

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:06.939
right? Knows it’s vulnerable to disease
and illness and death and the loss of things

00:13:06.939 --> 00:13:12.059
that they love. And the disintegration of
all things in some sense is—that’s enough

00:13:12.059 --> 00:13:17.959
of a problem to give you problems without
having to have been abused by it. And so the

00:13:17.959 --> 00:13:25.160
existentialists view life itself as a problem
of sufficient magnitude that it can destabilize

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:29.259
you mentally merely be being aware of it.

00:13:29.259 --> 00:13:33.859
And, so, they also believe that often what people
do as a consequence of that is flee from their

00:13:33.859 --> 00:13:38.199
own awareness. You know, so how do you deal
with the fact that life is suffering? Well,

00:13:38.199 --> 00:13:43.160
you don’t think about it. Well, for an existentialist,
that’s not a very good answer. And I would

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:47.389
also say, generally speaking, for clinical
psychologists that’s also not a very good

00:13:47.389 --> 00:13:53.720
answer because the literature suggests pretty
damn strongly that the harder you run from

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:59.660
something that you’re afraid of the larger
it becomes and the faster is chases you. So

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:05.549
avoidance of–avoidance is generally a very
bad strategy. Now it’s not always the case

00:14:05.549 --> 00:14:10.759
because sometimes you can’t do anything
about something, really you can’t. Well,

00:14:10.759 --> 00:14:15.769
there’s not a lot of sense spending a lot
of time planning and struggling against it,

00:14:15.769 --> 00:14:21.109
you know. And sometimes people are in generally
genuinely hopeless situations but often not.

00:14:21.109 --> 00:14:25.329
So, I really like the existentialists, man.
I think their—well all the people that we’re

00:14:25.329 --> 00:14:32.519
going to talk about—their thinking is extraordinarily
valuable. You know, it helps situate you in

00:14:32.519 --> 00:14:36.559
your own life. It helps clarify the nature
of your relationship to other people in the

00:14:36.559 --> 00:14:41.549
world. It helps you lay out what your future
could be if you were the creature that could

00:14:41.549 --> 00:14:43.329
be. It’s really wonderful.

00:14:43.329 --> 00:14:52.040
Phenomenologists: they’re a tricky bunch.
The fundamental phenomenologist was Martin

00:14:52.040 --> 00:15:00.399
Heidegger, who was also somewhat tangled up
with the Nazis, you know, which was clearly

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:04.439
not a good thing. And, you know, people debate
about whether or not you can separate his

00:15:04.439 --> 00:15:12.230
philosophy from what tangled him up from the
Nazis and I think you can. You know, so anyways,

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:21.309
Heidegger wanted to take a fresh look at the
nature of reality. And, so, Heidegger’s idea

00:15:21.309 --> 00:15:26.429
was we made a fundamental philosophical error
way back when we were Greek—Ancient Greek

00:15:26.429 --> 00:15:33.160
roughly speaking—and that we started concentrating
on the wrong elements of—he doesn’t really

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:39.439
call it reality; he calls it being. So a phenomenologist
is someone who’s concerned with being. And

00:15:39.439 --> 00:15:42.919
you might think, “Well what’s the difference
between being and reality?” And the answer

00:15:42.919 --> 00:15:48.809
to that is it depends on how you define being
and it depends on how you define reality.

00:15:48.809 --> 00:15:56.239
So, Heidegger’s observation was that, for
modern people roughly speaking, reality was

00:15:56.239 --> 00:16:04.459
sort of a deanimated material objective substrata.
It’s dead in its essence, roughly speaking,

00:16:04.459 --> 00:16:08.529
and the things that are most real are precisely
those things. You could think about that as

00:16:08.529 --> 00:16:14.509
objective reality for all intensive purposes.
But what Heidegger tried to point out is it’s

00:16:14.509 --> 00:16:20.489
a choice in some sense, because your knowledge
is finite, because you’re fundamentally

00:16:20.489 --> 00:16:25.669
ignorant about most things, you have to decide
that certain things are true and then act

00:16:25.669 --> 00:16:29.429
from that point. And, you know, Heidegger’s
point was: there’s a lot of things that

00:16:29.429 --> 00:16:35.259
you can decide about at the very fundamental
level of axiom. And so one of the things you

00:16:35.259 --> 00:16:39.989
can decide, for example, is whether or not
the objective world is what’s more real

00:16:39.989 --> 00:16:44.619
or whether or not your lived experience is
what’s more real.

00:16:44.619 --> 00:16:48.290
Now, you might think, “Well what’s the
distinction there?” And I would say the

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:54.720
distinction is probably something like watching
someone in pain on a video and being in pain

00:16:54.720 --> 00:17:04.010
yourself. You know, so pain’s a good example
of an element of being because it’s obviously—you

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:11.490
experience it with your own…it’s hard
to talk about just because he uses different

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:19.110
terminology. Pain is an element of your being.
And it’s not something—the pain itself

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:23.480
is not something that you can even conceptualize
in objective terms. Like you can measure it.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:26.449
You know, maybe your heart rate goes up when
you’re in pain or something like that. Or

00:17:26.449 --> 00:17:32.080
your facial expressions change. You know,
so you can index it but the indexes are not

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:37.940
the phenomena; that’s hence phenomenology,
right? And so phenomenologists have built

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:42.710
up an entire system of thinking predicated
on the initial axiom that the best way to

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:49.090
approach life is to assume that being is the
fundamental reality rather than the objective

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:51.450
as the fundamental level.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:54.309
And then you might think, “Well, why do
people bother with that sort of thing?”

00:17:54.309 --> 00:17:59.690
And part of it is: one of the—this is an
existentialist claim—one of the consequences

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:05.169
of developing the objective theory of existence
was obviously all the amazing technology we

00:18:05.169 --> 00:18:08.799
have around us and, you know, great, thank
God for that. Otherwise we’d be sitting

00:18:08.799 --> 00:18:14.529
outside freezing. But one of the consequences
of that is once people established objective

00:18:14.529 --> 00:18:20.000
reality and then defined it as ultimately
real, terrible questions of the meaning of

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:25.259
life arose. You know, if the world—the universe—is
made out of nothing but dead matter, it’s

00:18:25.259 --> 00:18:30.200
eternally existing, we’re nothing but a
candle flash in the wind, the idea creeps

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:35.470
into your mind that perhaps all the damn suffering
isn’t worth it. Or to put it another way,

00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:41.759
you know, does your life really have any real
meaning or is that just an illusion. Well,

00:18:41.759 --> 00:18:45.850
Heidegger would say the reason that you have
that problem is because you’re approaching

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:51.309
the entire problem wrong. Clearly, things have
meaning; they just don’t if you make the

00:18:51.309 --> 00:18:57.399
hypothesis that what’s objective is ultimately
real and everything else is merely a secondary

00:18:57.399 --> 00:19:04.539
consequence of that. So, anyways, it’s complicated.
It takes a while to get your head around it.

00:19:04.539 --> 00:19:09.070
The humanists and the existentialists and
to some degree the phenomenologists: they

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:14.409
all came out of—there was a variety of sources
for their thought and I would say one of them

00:19:14.409 --> 00:19:19.850
is the clash between religion and science
or between value and objective truth. And

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:24.490
they’re trying to rectify the division.
And Piaget himself was trying to do some of

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:29.779
that as well. So was Jung I guess. And the
other thing is a lot of the existentialist

00:19:29.779 --> 00:19:35.759
and humanist work was post-World War II and
so, you know, after World War II happened

00:19:35.759 --> 00:19:39.820
with its immense catastrophes, of course people
who were thinking about human nature were

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:44.090
very interested in why such a thing could
happen. And existentialism, at least as a

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:50.580
psychological school arose in part as a reaction
to the horrors of World War II. It’s a very

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:56.149
serious branch of psychology and it mostly
characterized thinking in the 1950’s before

00:19:56.149 --> 00:20:00.679
the hippies came along and, you know, made
everything sweetness and light, so—which

00:20:00.679 --> 00:20:03.840
is more where Rogers and Maslow fit.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:09.240
So, we’re going to talk about them a fair
bit—the three different categories. It’s

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:13.090
kind of a strange approach in some sense that
this class takes because I talked to you about

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:19.029
some psychologists in that period but the
other people I talked to you mostly about,

00:20:19.029 --> 00:20:24.799
they’re not technically psychologists—depends
on what you mean by psychologist after all.

00:20:24.799 --> 00:20:29.990
I talk a lot about Nietzsche and Dostoevsky
and also a lot about a Russian literary figure,

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:36.240
Alexander Solzhenitsyn, who wrote a book called
“The Gulag Archipelago” which was one of

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:42.429
the—there was a few cataclysmic events that
brought the totalitarian system of the Soviet

00:20:42.429 --> 00:20:49.080
Union to a halt. And you can argue about what
they were but one of them for sure was Solzhenitsyn’s

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:55.779
publication of “The Gulag Archipelago”
which is a 2700 word book—2700 page book,

00:20:55.779 --> 00:21:02.860
got to get that right—that describes what
it was like to be there and how many people

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:10.399
died. And it’s a searing book. He won a
Nobel Prize and he deserved it. I mean, Solzhenitsyn:

00:21:10.399 --> 00:21:15.409
a remarkable person. He’s like the Dostoevsky
of the twentieth century and that’s really

00:21:15.409 --> 00:21:20.960
saying something. And Dostoevsky’s another
person I use a lot in that section. And both

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:25.070
Dostoevsky and Nietzsche regarded themselves
as psychologists fundamentally.

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:28.919
And, you know, it’s funny because when you
think about scientists, you know, you usually

00:21:28.919 --> 00:21:33.389
think about people who are conducting experiments,
right? But, then, one of the things—and you’ll

00:21:33.389 --> 00:21:37.850
notice this if you think about it because
a lot of you have taken a fair number of science

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:42.570
courses of one form or another. And, you know,
a lot of what you’re taught when you’re

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:47.389
taught how to do science is how to generate,
you know, how to lay out an experiment properly,

00:21:47.389 --> 00:21:51.509
how to get the methodology right, how to do
the analysis properly, maybe how to structure

00:21:51.509 --> 00:21:58.480
the revealing of your findings in proper scientific
format, you know, and how to test a hypothesis.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:01.289
But one of the things that you’re never
really told about is, well, how the hell do

00:22:01.289 --> 00:22:06.500
you generate the hypothesis to begin with
because, you know, really that’s a least

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:11.909
half the problem and it’s probably a lot
more than half the problem. And, so, part of

00:22:11.909 --> 00:22:17.590
being a scientist is the ability to be a hypothesis
generator, not just a hypothesis tester. Maybe

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:24.590
you need to be both to be a true scientist
but maybe not. Maybe you can just generate

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:29.740
extraordinary hypotheses and it’s certainly
the case that people like Dostoevsky and Nietzsche

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:33.190
were unbelievably good hypothesis generators.

00:22:33.190 --> 00:22:38.429
And so, you know, their thinking in many ways
underlies the entire development of thought—the

00:22:38.429 --> 00:22:41.830
sort of thought that you guys are going to
encounter in university anyways—their thinking

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:47.659
underlies the entire development of the edifice
of I would say both scientific and nonscientific

00:22:47.659 --> 00:22:53.429
thought across the entire twentieth century.
And, so, it’s quite entertaining to go down

00:22:53.429 --> 00:22:58.210
there and, you know, wander around the roots
of these sorts of things because you find

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:02.460
out how things are linked together and why
they’re linked together and how they developed

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:07.230
and what that means. And, also, what it means
for you to have those ideas and why you have

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.620
them and what role they play in your life.

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:16.279
So, okay, so, after that, that’s pretty much—in
some sense, you might think that’s where

00:23:16.279 --> 00:23:24.549
the clinical and philosophical part of the
course ends. Now, I’m a clinical psychologist

00:23:24.549 --> 00:23:28.879
and most of the people who were great personality
theorists were clinical psychologists. And

00:23:28.879 --> 00:23:33.350
that explains to some degree why the first
part of the course is philosophical. Because

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:37.259
the thing about clinical psychologists and
psychiatrists and medical doctors for that

00:23:37.259 --> 00:23:44.559
matter is that they’re not exactly scientists;
they’re more like engineers. So, what an

00:23:44.559 --> 00:23:49.330
engineer wants to do—an engineer might use
scientific knowledge and, you know, good for

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:53.169
them; they should. But really what they want
to do is make something change. They want

00:23:53.169 --> 00:23:59.379
to build something, they want to transform
something, they want to act on a beneficial—generally—in

00:23:59.379 --> 00:24:05.249
a beneficial way. Well, the same thing is
the case with the health domains. It’s like

00:24:05.249 --> 00:24:09.899
someone comes to you with a psychological
problem. What you want to do is you want to

00:24:09.899 --> 00:24:16.379
make it better and better is not a scientific
category. So, to the degree that you’re a—like

00:24:16.379 --> 00:24:20.230
you can say well it’s scientific because
worse means abnormal. It’s like, that’s

00:24:20.230 --> 00:24:24.700
not right. You can make that case but it’s
shallow because there’s lots of times when

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:31.149
you don’t consider normal healthy. You know,
you consider exceptional healthy, right? Or

00:24:31.149 --> 00:24:36.460
maybe you consider exceptional desirable and
desirable might even be better than healthy,

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:36.960
right?

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:41.159
So, you can’t get out of the value domain
when you’re trying to interact with people

00:24:41.159 --> 00:24:46.850
and sort out their lives. Because partly what
you’re always asking is: “What’s wrong?”

00:24:46.850 --> 00:24:50.789
“What’s the matter?” “Why are you
dissatisfied?” “Why are you suffering?”

00:24:50.789 --> 00:24:55.039
“Why aren’t you productive?” “Why
do you fight with everyone?” You know, “Why

00:24:55.039 --> 00:25:00.440
can’t you sleep at night?” “Why do you
wake up screaming?” You know, “Why can’t

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:05.360
you pursue anything that’s to your own benefit?”
“Why do you hurt everyone that you know?”

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:09.419
You know, those are moral questions. And then
the next problem is: “Okay, well what are

00:25:09.419 --> 00:25:12.590
we going to do about it?” “What do you
want?” “How would you like your life to

00:25:12.590 --> 00:25:15.539
look if it could look the way you wanted it
to?” And then, “How are we going to get

00:25:15.539 --> 00:25:20.129
from where you are to that?” And those are
all—they’re engineering questions in a

00:25:20.129 --> 00:25:25.009
sense but above all they’re ethical questions
because they deal with how to act. So, you

00:25:25.009 --> 00:25:30.340
know, I don’t want to just slide over that.
Because that’s generally what happens because

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:34.309
physicians and clinical psychologists and
say psychiatrists—even though they’re

00:25:34.309 --> 00:25:38.100
physicians—they like to think that what
they’re doing science and that’s not right.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:43.289
It’s informed by science, but that’s not
the same thing. It’s particularly true with

00:25:43.289 --> 00:25:46.990
doctors because they’re not even trained
to be scientists.

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:54.210
So, okay so that’s that. And then the next
part of the course—the last half—it concentrates

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:59.200
on two elements of personality theory; more
modern elements I would say although not more

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:07.669
valuable necessarily because of that. Partly
very neurobiological, because we’ve made

00:26:07.669 --> 00:26:17.470
a lot of strides in the last 50—100 years
let’s say. In understanding the structure

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:23.980
and the function of the brain. Like the things
we don’t know about the brain, man, it’s—there

00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:28.749
are lots of things we don’t understand about
the brain. We have no idea, for example, what

00:26:28.749 --> 00:26:34.490
its relationship is to conscientiousness except
more or less it seems necessary. You know,

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:39.139
so even that you can dispute to some degree
because there are great case histories of

00:26:39.139 --> 00:26:44.559
people who were born with hydrocephalus. And
so that means that your skull basically fills

00:26:44.559 --> 00:26:51.570
with water. And the most famous case of that
was a French guy who went for an MRI for God

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:55.519
only knows what reason—it had nothing to
do with what was eventually discovered—but

00:26:55.519 --> 00:27:00.749
he only had five percent of his cortical tissue.
His whole brain—his whole head was filled

00:27:00.749 --> 00:27:05.440
with cerebrospinal fluid and the brain was
just a little skin around the side that was

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:08.600
like a little balloon. And as far as they
could tell there was nothing wrong with him.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:12.529
It’s like, “Hmm, well isn’t that interesting?
What the hell’s all the rest of that brain

00:27:12.529 --> 00:27:17.870
for?” And then you get the other issue—like
I don’t know if you’ve ever heard of (can’t

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:24.169
remember her name…I think it’s Pepper…)
She had a famous parrot—an African grey.

00:27:24.169 --> 00:27:30.179
You can look it up: talking African grey parrot.
And that damn parrot, man, that thing could

00:27:30.179 --> 00:27:35.190
talk like a four-year-old kid. Way better
than any chimpanzee. Full sentences, it seemed

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:40.769
to know what it was doing. It’s like, it’s
a parrot, right? Its brain is this big!

00:27:40.769 --> 00:27:42.419
So, how is that even possible?

00:27:42.419 --> 00:27:48.360
So, okay so there’s lots of things we don’t
know about the brain really. And it’s unbelievably

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:53.330
complicated. So, but we have learned a lot
of things that are interesting. We kind of

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:59.259
understand the existence and the location
of the fundamental reward and pain and anxiety

00:27:59.259 --> 00:28:03.610
circuits. That’s a big deal. Those are like
discovering major continents, you know? We

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:08.450
understand to some degree how people learn
and under what conditions they learn and we

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:15.710
understand, I would say to some degree, what
the fundamental motivations and emotions are.

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:20.169
Now those are not scientific categories: motivations
and emotions. They’re linguistic categories

00:28:20.169 --> 00:28:25.600
but we’re stuck with them. So, but we know
a fair bit about their underlying neurobiological

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:30.519
basis and I want to take you through that
in some detail because for a bunch of reasons.

00:28:30.519 --> 00:28:36.749
Partly, because it’s provides a nice scientific
underpinning to a lot of the hypotheses and

00:28:36.749 --> 00:28:41.529
presuppositions of the psychoanalysts, you
know? So, for example, Freud hypothesized

00:28:41.529 --> 00:28:46.929
the existence of the id. Now there is no id
in your brain but it doesn’t really matter

00:28:46.929 --> 00:28:51.509
because the id—the idea of the id—is a
functional category; it’s like a tool. But

00:28:51.509 --> 00:28:56.220
there are lots of id-like structures in your
brain. You know and one of them in particular

00:28:56.220 --> 00:29:00.269
is the hypothalamus and that thing—like
human beings like to think that their really

00:29:00.269 --> 00:29:05.330
impressive cortical cap is the thing that’s,
you know, in charge of things. But your hypothalamus,

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:09.330
which is this little tiny thing that sits
at the top of your spinal chord, man, that’s

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:15.889
what running you. You know, as long as you’re
not hungry or not angry or not chasing after

00:29:15.889 --> 00:29:21.389
sexual excitement and not gambling and not
addicted; so, as long as you’re completely

00:29:21.389 --> 00:29:27.889
well fed and warm and comfortable, then you
can think and you’re more or less in control.

00:29:27.889 --> 00:29:32.559
But as soon as any of those things go astray
it’s subcortical all the way.

00:29:32.559 --> 00:29:37.360
You know, and you can see this, for example,
in conditions like anorexia. You know, because

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:41.090
anorexia—you could think of anorexia as
a war between the cortex and they hypothalamus.

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:47.570
And I’ll tell you man, the only time the
cortex wins is when the anorexic dies, because

00:29:47.570 --> 00:29:50.970
other than that it’s the hypothalamus that
wins all the time, which is why anorexics

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:55.779
cannot stop themselves from binging. So, you
know, so they’ll cut their body weight down

00:29:55.779 --> 00:30:00.779
to say seventy five percent of what it should
be and that’s hard. Like you really—it’s

00:30:00.779 --> 00:30:06.240
not easy to starve yourself like that. But
they’ll still binge like mad and, you know,

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:09.960
eat a whole gallon of ice cream and two bags
of bread. And they can’t stop themselves

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:13.179
and that’s because your hypothalamus, it
would rather you didn’t starve to death.

00:30:13.179 --> 00:30:17.860
And if you’re not paying attention, if you’re
not on top of the game or maybe if you’ve

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:23.230
been hurt recently and so your resources or
your—what would you say?—the negative

00:30:23.230 --> 00:30:27.940
emotion has been interfering with your cognitive
capacity, the hypothalamus will come up and

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:32.029
grab you and say, “Man, you’re hungry
and it’s off to the fridge with you.”

00:30:32.029 --> 00:30:35.929
You know, so two in the morning you’re going
to be eating everything in there in a kind

00:30:35.929 --> 00:30:38.409
of a daze. And you’ll snap out of that and
think, “Wow, what the hell was that?”

00:30:38.409 --> 00:30:43.820
It was like well, that’s the id, folks,
and it’s no trivial thing. And it’s a

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:48.429
good thing too because if it didn’t tell
you what to do all the time—like think about

00:30:48.429 --> 00:30:53.129
how foolish you are. If you had to breathe
voluntarily how many days do you think you’d

00:30:53.129 --> 00:30:57.269
go through? Eh, it’s like you’d wake up
one day and an hour later you’d forget to

00:30:57.269 --> 00:31:04.399
breathe and then you’d be dead. So, like
your brain doesn’t let you do anything important;

00:31:04.399 --> 00:31:07.950
it takes care of all that in the background,
you know, because you’re just not that smart.

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:12.269
I mean, you don’t know how to digest things,
for example. You actually don’t know how

00:31:12.269 --> 00:31:18.470
you walk even though you can do it. So, there
are fundamental subsystems, some of them aren’t

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:22.159
even conscious, weirdly enough, even though
they’re neurological: they’re doing all

00:31:22.159 --> 00:31:26.489
the heavy lifting. And like your cortex is
along there—it’s along for the ride in

00:31:26.489 --> 00:31:31.239
some sense. And it sort of thinks its in control
but yeah, no. Not really.

00:31:31.239 --> 00:31:35.720
Okay, so we’re going to talk a fair bit
about fundamental brain structures and the

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:40.139
function of those structures because I like
functional neuroanatomy. I like—I don’t

00:31:40.139 --> 00:31:43.399
want to know just what the brain is made out
of but I want know what all the parts are

00:31:43.399 --> 00:31:49.360
doing. And so, there’s difficult papers
in there; Swanson, that’s a hard paper,

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:56.460
and the paper by Gray. Those are papers that
were written by I think genius-level neuropsychologists

00:31:56.460 --> 00:32:00.669
and, you know, they put their whole life’s
work into a paper and so, you know, you’re

00:32:00.669 --> 00:32:03.600
going to pick it up and you’re going to
think, “Wow, this is hard.” It’s like,

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:08.529
“Yeah! Some genius spent thirty years writing
it. It’s going to be hard.” But they’re

00:32:08.529 --> 00:32:13.809
so informative; they’re worth the struggle
if that’s the sort of thing that you want

00:32:13.809 --> 00:32:15.100
to struggle with.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:20.239
So, that’s the biological end and then there’s
the psychometric end. And psychometrics—very

00:32:20.239 --> 00:32:24.119
unpopular field of psychology, even among
psychologists, and the reason for that is

00:32:24.119 --> 00:32:30.269
that psychometricians, I guess, have actually
discovered things and people hate what they’ve

00:32:30.269 --> 00:32:34.999
discovered. So, and no wonder. So, for example,
it was people who do psychometrics, engineers

00:32:34.999 --> 00:32:41.539
actually who entered psychology, who discovered
intelligence. Technical psychometric intelligence.

00:32:41.539 --> 00:32:46.279
IQ. And people will tell you there’s lots
of kinds of intelligence. It’s like, “No

00:32:46.279 --> 00:32:53.440
there aren’t. That’s wrong.” And it’s
wrong in this way: the statistics that psychologists

00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:58.210
use were invented by the people who discovered
IQ. And that means that every single thing

00:32:58.210 --> 00:33:03.590
that a psychologist has ever claimed that’s
been verified in some way using some statistical

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:09.289
process has been verified the same way that
IQ was verified. And so you don’t get to

00:33:09.289 --> 00:33:13.220
say, “Well the IQ stuff’s invalid,”
without saying all of psychology—and a lot

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:17.159
of other fields—is invalid too because it’s
the same methods of proof. And it’s even

00:33:17.159 --> 00:33:22.340
worse than that because like the relationship
between IQ and life success is way stronger

00:33:22.340 --> 00:33:27.720
than the relationship between almost any other
psychological phenomena and almost anything

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:34.549
else. So, for example, for you guys, even
though, you know, you’re all far above average

00:33:34.549 --> 00:33:38.460
in intelligence—because otherwise you wouldn’t
be here—like at least eighty-fifth percentile.

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:41.249
And that’s something to think about because
a lot of that is determined by biological

00:33:41.249 --> 00:33:44.149
factors so, you know, so a huge chunk of the
reason that you’re here is because, you

00:33:44.149 --> 00:33:49.669
know, you won part of the genetic lottery.
So like hooray for you. And no one screwed

00:33:49.669 --> 00:33:55.009
it up too badly as you were growing up. You
know, but even among you guys, the correlation

00:33:55.009 --> 00:34:00.039
between your intelligence and your grade point
average is quite high even though it’s a

00:34:00.039 --> 00:34:03.730
truncated sample, right, because you’re
all pretty bright. And even though there’s

00:34:03.730 --> 00:34:09.210
a lot of error in the grade measurement system
because it doesn’t only index intelligence,

00:34:09.210 --> 00:34:16.309
it’s still very powerful. And, so, if you
controlled for conscientiousness and other

00:34:16.309 --> 00:34:22.159
elements of personality, which you can do
statistically, in twenty years the ten percent

00:34:22.159 --> 00:34:25.690
of the people in this room who have the highest
IQs will have the highest income virtually

00:34:25.690 --> 00:34:30.270
certainly. You know, unless some terrible
thing happens to them. You can overcome it

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:34.419
to some degree if you’re really hard working.
And that’s conscientiousness, you know,

00:34:34.419 --> 00:34:38.510
but that’s also a trait which seems to have
quite a heavy biological loading.

00:34:38.510 --> 00:34:43.139
So, well so that’s the sort of thing that
people hate about psychometricians because

00:34:43.139 --> 00:34:48.869
they come up with these facts and they’re
facts that are really dismal. And I can give

00:34:48.869 --> 00:34:55.809
you an example: so you guys all have an IQ
of over 115 roughly speaking. 100 makes you

00:34:55.809 --> 00:35:00.839
about as smart as the typical high school
graduate. You know, 115—that’s kind of

00:35:00.839 --> 00:35:09.349
the low end for managing the rigors of a fairly
high-end university. You know, 115, you’re

00:35:09.349 --> 00:35:13.760
still going to have a lot of trouble; you’re
going to have to work like mad. So, but okay

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:20.950
so 115: now, that’s one standard deviation
above the mean. One standard deviation below

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:24.710
the mean is an IQ of 85. Now there’s just
as many people out there in the world who

00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:32.030
have an IQ of 85 as there are people who are
able to go to college. Okay, so then you think,

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:38.869
“What can you do if you have an IQ of 85?”
And, well, here’s one answer: so, the US Army

00:35:38.869 --> 00:35:43.220
has been doing psychometric testing for a
hundred and some years for a whole bunch of

00:35:43.220 --> 00:35:48.260
reasons but they’ve done a lot of the basic
science, you know. And one thing about the

00:35:48.260 --> 00:35:53.410
US Army is they like to have recruits. And
you can understand why because do you really

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:57.400
want to go off somewhere and get shot? And
the answer to that is generally no. So it’s

00:35:57.400 --> 00:36:01.950
not like there are people lining up to get
into the army. Even in peace times it’s

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:07.510
hard to get people. And also, the Americans
have used the US Army as a—you know, imagine

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:12.460
you have an underclass of people who aren’t
doing well, which is the case in every society,

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:16.780
and you want to boost some of them up, you
know, into something resembling the middle

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:21.329
class. You can do that fairly effectively
by inducting young men in particular into

00:36:21.329 --> 00:36:25.020
the army because they get some training and,
you know, they get some discipline and maybe

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:29.069
they can kind of clamber up into the middle
class. And so there’s a lot of policy reasons

00:36:29.069 --> 00:36:35.410
A) why the American Army armed forces want
people, and B) you know, just to be in the

00:36:35.410 --> 00:36:40.400
army but also for social reasons—positive
social reasons. So, they’re not likely not

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:48.049
to let you in. The consequence of hundred
years of IQ testing was the establishment

00:36:48.049 --> 00:36:52.770
of a law in the United States: you cannot
induct anyone into the army; you cannot accept

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:58.720
them into the army if they have an IQ of less
than 82. That’s ten percent of the population.

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:06.030
So you think about that. That means one person
in ten cannot do anything that is of positive

00:37:06.030 --> 00:37:12.349
value in an organization that’s rather complex
but not more complex than other organizations

00:37:12.349 --> 00:37:18.619
that’s desperate for people. Nothing they
can offer has positive value. It’s worse

00:37:18.619 --> 00:37:23.270
to have them in the army than not to have
them there at all. Really, now, you know,

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:29.569
that’s a terrible way of putting it but
it’s a horrible statistic. Because it also

00:37:29.569 --> 00:37:33.990
means, you know, if you think about the army
as roughly equivalent to world in some sense

00:37:33.990 --> 00:37:38.579
in terms of it’s complexity, it means that
there’s ten percent of the population who

00:37:38.579 --> 00:37:44.490
just cannot really exist well in a complex
cognitive society. So, and you might say,

00:37:44.490 --> 00:37:47.440
“Well, you know, something should be done
about that.” It’s like, “Good luck”.

00:37:47.440 --> 00:37:51.180
People have been trying to do something about
that for a very long time. Like, there’s

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:57.640
been billions, if not trillions, of dollars
poured into attempts to do cognitive remediation

00:37:57.640 --> 00:38:00.460
in the United States. Well, not to mention
that we actually have an education system

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:04.950
and the goal is actually to make people smarter.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:10.250
So, we’re going to talk a lot about psychometrics
and the thing about the psychometricians is

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:14.930
they’re basically brute force statisticians.
In some ways they’re atheoretical. You know,

00:38:14.930 --> 00:38:20.089
and they use computer power, roughly speaking,
and advanced statistical techniques to look

00:38:20.089 --> 00:38:24.890
at large data sets and extract out patterns.
And, you know, I was—I hated the Big Five

00:38:24.890 --> 00:38:28.309
when I first came on it. I thought, “Oh
God, this is boring.” It’s like, there’s

00:38:28.309 --> 00:38:33.640
no theory behind it; it’s not prescriptive;
it’s not interesting in any literary manner,

00:38:33.640 --> 00:38:40.510
like it doesn’t have a good story; it came
out of—it’s so dry. But, you know, it

00:38:40.510 --> 00:38:49.319
works and that’s how it is. So, we’re going
to spend a lot of time on psychometrics and,

00:38:49.319 --> 00:38:53.690
you know, my lab too has done a fair bit of
work over the last ten years elaborating out

00:38:53.690 --> 00:38:58.690
the Big Five Model. So one of the things we’ll
talk about is the Big Ten Model and what it

00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:03.369
does, roughly speaking, is break each of the
five into two—we call them aspects. And

00:39:03.369 --> 00:39:07.549
they’re pretty interesting. Like they predict
political belief, for example, quite nicely.

00:39:07.549 --> 00:39:12.970
So, conservatives tend to be more orderly,
which is part of conscientiousness and less

00:39:12.970 --> 00:39:18.160
creative, less open. And liberals tend to
be more open and less conscientious. Which

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:22.380
a cynic would say that’s why they’re so
into income redistribution: because they actually

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:26.750
don’t work. So, you know, the conservatives
work like mad and the liberals say, “Hey

00:39:26.750 --> 00:39:30.260
hey, how about some of that money?” You
know, and, “The system’s unfair.” And

00:39:30.260 --> 00:39:34.410
the conservatives say, “Well no. We worked
hard and we’re not giving you any.” Well,

00:39:34.410 --> 00:39:39.920
you know, they both have flaws in their theories.
The liberals, by the way, make very good entrepreneurs.

00:39:39.920 --> 00:39:47.049
Like, no let me rephrase that. Most entrepreneurs
are liberal in their political outlook and

00:39:47.049 --> 00:39:50.720
that’s because they’re high in openness.
So the way it really seems to work is that

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:56.520
liberal-minded people start businesses and
conservatives run them. So, they’re necessary

00:39:56.520 --> 00:40:00.770
for one other. Yeah, well because the liberals,
being open, they’re off to the next idea,

00:40:00.770 --> 00:40:04.339
you know, and they don’t want to attempt
to the damn details; they get bored of that.

00:40:04.339 --> 00:40:08.289
They’re not particularly orderly, you know.
But once you’ve established a process and

00:40:08.289 --> 00:40:12.089
it works you need someone to watch to make
sure the process keeps working. Well that’s

00:40:12.089 --> 00:40:15.380
not very interesting if you’re a creative
person.

00:40:15.380 --> 00:40:29.910
So, okay, so, that’s basically that. That’s
the course. So, now, what have we got for time

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:33.980
here? So I think what I’ll do now is I’m
going tell you a story and you’re going

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:39.420
to think it has absolutely nothing to do with
what we’ve been talking about, but that’s

00:40:39.420 --> 00:40:49.740
okay. That’s okay. So I picked this weird
picture. It’s a picture of Jonah—biblical

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:58.890
character. It’s a 2500-year-old story so
that’s a pretty old story. And generally

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:03.430
if a story is 2500 years old it’s a lot
older than that. You know, it just happened

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:08.030
to be written down 2500 years ago, but, you
know, that long ago it wasn’t like there

00:41:08.030 --> 00:41:12.440
were novels and books and that you could find
fifty different stories, you know, at your

00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:16.579
fingertips. There were a few stories and everyone
knew them. And God only knows how long they’d

00:41:16.579 --> 00:41:21.970
been around but it was a very very very very
long time. So, this was written down 2500 years

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:28.309
ago but we don’t really have any idea how
old it is. And, in some sense, it’s almost

00:41:28.309 --> 00:41:34.079
infinitely old, at least on any human scale.
Now, you see this picture and what you see

00:41:34.079 --> 00:41:40.230
is this thing that—what would you call it?
Well, it’s a medieval idea of a whale is

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:43.010
what it is. You know, and you might think,
“Well, that isn’t really what a whale

00:41:43.010 --> 00:41:48.119
looks like,” and that’s kind of right
but a medieval whale wasn’t the whale that

00:41:48.119 --> 00:41:55.599
we have. Like, whale in medieval times meant
large terrifying underwater thing. You know,

00:41:55.599 --> 00:41:59.609
and, so, maybe that was—maybe a giant squid
was a whale and a whale was a whale and a

00:41:59.609 --> 00:42:04.900
shark was a whale and, you know, the category’s
not the same. So, that kind of accounts for

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:11.579
the—that in pure ignorance is what accounts
for the kind of strange mythologized representation.

00:42:11.579 --> 00:42:16.220
But it’s still a representation of something;
it’s just a little generalized. Now what

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:22.859
he’s doing is vomiting Jonah up onto dry
land. So, and you think, “That’s pretty

00:42:22.859 --> 00:42:23.750
damn weird.”

00:42:23.750 --> 00:42:29.339
How many have you seen Pinocchio? That’s
a lot, eh? How many of you haven’t? Really?

00:42:29.339 --> 00:42:33.500
You haven’t seen Pinocchio? What kind of
parents did you have anyways?

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:34.619
[Students’ laughter]

00:42:34.619 --> 00:42:39.589
Okay, well, you remember in Pinocchio that
Pinocchio’s father is stuck in a whale,

00:42:39.589 --> 00:42:44.480
eh, and then Pinocchio has to go down there
and rescue him. And one of the things you

00:42:44.480 --> 00:42:48.760
didn’t notice when you were watching Pinocchio
is just how daft that is. There you are sitting

00:42:48.760 --> 00:42:55.010
there, you’re watching a puppet go down
into the ocean to rescue his father from a

00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:58.270
whale. And you think, “Oh, that makes sense.”

00:42:58.270 --> 00:42:59.049
[Students’ laughter]

00:42:59.049 --> 00:43:03.010
It’s like, well also that’s interesting,
right? That’s interesting. Why does that

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:11.220
make sense? Why does that make sense? Because
it doesn’t make any sense. But see that’s

00:43:11.220 --> 00:43:16.500
a good example of the grip of any archetypal
idea because an archetypal idea is a very

00:43:16.500 --> 00:43:21.000
deep and old idea that makes sense to you
even though if someone points out to you that

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:25.369
you believe, which you more or less do while
you’re watching a movie, you think, “Oh

00:43:25.369 --> 00:43:29.230
yeah, it’s really weird that I believe that.”
It’s like, you know, and that isn’t a

00:43:29.230 --> 00:43:34.339
puppet going down into the ocean to rescue
his father from a whale either, just so you

00:43:34.339 --> 00:43:38.289
know it. It’s a drawing of a puppet going
down into the ocean to rescue his father from

00:43:38.289 --> 00:43:44.970
a whale so it’s like divorced from reality
in all sorts of ways. But what’s so interesting

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:49.170
is you go into the theatre and watch something
like that and if someone taps you on the shoulder

00:43:49.170 --> 00:43:52.640
and say, “Hey hey, do you know what you’re
doing?” You say, “Why don’t you shut

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:54.079
up and let me watch the movie.”

00:43:54.079 --> 00:43:54.619
[Students’ laughter]

00:43:54.619 --> 00:43:58.200
Right, you don’t notice, “Oh yes I’m
very peculiar. I’m a very peculiar thing

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:02.710
sitting here watching this and not noticing
how strange I am.” You know, and so well,

00:44:02.710 --> 00:44:10.010
that’s partly why I’m going to tell you
this story, because it’s true even though

00:44:10.010 --> 00:44:11.230
it isn’t.

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:17.869
You know, and that’s a funny thing. I had
a very very strange professor at the University

00:44:17.869 --> 00:44:21.970
of Alberta. He died a few years ago. He got
on a bus, I think it was in Winnipeg, and

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:27.720
he never got off the bus and no one ever say
him again. So, he had been a prison psychologist.

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:31.599
He worked in the maximum-security prison in
the University of Alberta so who knows, maybe

00:44:31.599 --> 00:44:37.539
he met one of his old clients. You know, it’s
possible but it’s very strange—very strange

00:44:37.539 --> 00:44:44.890
ending. Anyways, one time we were talking
about fiction, you know, and he said, “Fiction

00:44:44.890 --> 00:44:49.260
is a lie that can tell you the truth.” And
that’s a very interesting way of thinking

00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:55.599
about it, you know, because you read fiction
and, you know, it’s true. But it’s not

00:44:55.599 --> 00:45:00.530
obviously because it’s not really about
a thing that actually happened. But it’s

00:45:00.530 --> 00:45:04.079
more than that in some ways because if it’s
good fiction it’s about a whole bunch of

00:45:04.079 --> 00:45:10.030
things that happened sort of like that. Right,
it’s a distillation. It’s like someone

00:45:10.030 --> 00:45:15.680
tells you a long story and then, you know,
you talk to your partner and they ask you,

00:45:15.680 --> 00:45:18.730
“What was that all about?” And then you
tell them in two sentences what it was about.

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:24.780
You boil it down to the gist, right? Well
a really good story is the gist of something.

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:29.930
And some of the really old stories, the really
weird ones like Jonah and the Whale, for example—which

00:45:29.930 --> 00:45:34.880
as I said is told to some degree in the story
of Pinocchio—they’re the gist of a lot

00:45:34.880 --> 00:45:41.180
of things and people have been boiling those
damn stories down for a very very very very

00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:47.700
very very long period of time. Thousands of
people over thousands of years and they come

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:52.730
up with these weird stories that people remember.
And then people think, “Well, they’re

00:45:52.730 --> 00:45:55.460
real.” It’s like, “Yeah they are but
they’re not real in the way a scientific

00:45:55.460 --> 00:45:57.650
story is real. It’s a different kind of
real.”

00:45:57.650 --> 00:46:06.569
So here’s the story of Jonah. And so it’s
an old Jewish story. There’s probably a

00:46:06.569 --> 00:46:13.430
variant of it in the Quaran, but I don’t
know that variant so and maybe there isn’t.

00:46:13.430 --> 00:46:20.240
Anyways, Jonah lives in 500 BC and God has
a chat with him one day and he says, “Look

00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:26.650
Jonah, there’s this city, Nineveh, and people
there, you know, they’re really not on the

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:31.089
straight and narrow and things are going to
go to hell in a hand basket very rapidly if

00:46:31.089 --> 00:46:34.510
someone doesn’t do something about that.
So, I think that you should go to Nineveh and

00:46:34.510 --> 00:46:37.920
you should tell the king and all the people
that are there that, you know, really they’re

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:42.520
just not doing the right thing”. And Jonah,
being a perfectly intelligent person, says,

00:46:42.520 --> 00:46:47.980
“You want me to go to a city and you want
me to go tell the king, like this authoritarian

00:46:47.980 --> 00:46:51.500
guy who has absolute power over everything,
that there’s something wrong with what he’s

00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:55.930
doing that all his citizens are corrupt? That’s
what you want me to do?” And Jonah says,

00:46:55.930 --> 00:47:01.420
“There’s no way I’m doing that!” because
as I said he’s a smart guy. So he runs off

00:47:01.420 --> 00:47:06.990
and he goes to this town and as he’s running
off he rents a boat more or less. And it’s

00:47:06.990 --> 00:47:10.030
a big boat and there are fishermen on it.
And they’re going to like row him something

00:47:10.030 --> 00:47:15.200
that God can’t find him, which is not going
to be an easy thing. And, so, God of course

00:47:15.200 --> 00:47:19.599
can’t be fooled by such relatively poor
strategic choices because he’s omniscient

00:47:19.599 --> 00:47:20.380
and all that.

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:24.200
And, so, he’s out on the water, Jonah, and
there’s the fisherman there and then so

00:47:24.200 --> 00:47:28.730
God calls up this big storm. And the fisherman
are shorting out about this because they’re

00:47:28.730 --> 00:47:32.000
going to swamp the boat and then they’re
all going to die and they’re not that happy

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:36.799
about that. So, what they decide to do is throw
everything overboard so that the boat is lightened.

00:47:36.799 --> 00:47:40.309
But that doesn’t help much because the storm
gets worse. And so then they all of them start

00:47:40.309 --> 00:47:44.339
praying. And they all have different gods
so, you know, it’s like a little god competition.

00:47:44.339 --> 00:47:49.440
And one fisherman talks to one god and another
fisherman talks to another god and nothing’s

00:47:49.440 --> 00:47:53.970
happening. So, Jonah happens to be asleep in
the hold of the ship. Now, he’s already told

00:47:53.970 --> 00:47:58.170
them that he’s been running from his god
and so they think, “Ha, we better go wake

00:47:58.170 --> 00:48:01.609
up Jonah because maybe he’s the guy that’s
causing all the trouble.” So, they go down

00:48:01.609 --> 00:48:06.410
there and they wake him up and they ask him
about his god. And he says, “Well my god

00:48:06.410 --> 00:48:10.569
made the heavens and the earth,” and their
gods, they weren’t doing anything that magnificent

00:48:10.569 --> 00:48:13.420
so they’re pretty freaked out about this.
And so they told Jonah it’s like, “What

00:48:13.420 --> 00:48:17.619
the hell are you running away from this guy
for? It’s not a good idea.” And Jonah

00:48:17.619 --> 00:48:20.700
says, “Yeah, yeah, I’m sure it’s my fault
that this storm has come up so why don’t

00:48:20.700 --> 00:48:24.960
you just throw me over.” And they say, “Well
we can’t really do that because, you know,

00:48:24.960 --> 00:48:28.829
you shouldn’t run away like that, but you’re
not a murderer or anything; we can’t just

00:48:28.829 --> 00:48:33.250
toss you over.” And so they decide to try
to row to shore but God’s not having any

00:48:33.250 --> 00:48:37.750
of that. So, he raises up a big storm and it’s
getting worse and worse and finally the fisherman

00:48:37.750 --> 00:48:42.170
say, “Ah, you know, over you go.” And
Jonah’s into that because he knows he’s

00:48:42.170 --> 00:48:47.950
a bad guy. And, so, a whale swallows him. Now
you think, “Hmm, that’s probably not exactly

00:48:47.950 --> 00:48:51.890
right because first, if a whale swallowed
you then you’d be dead.” And it was probably

00:48:51.890 --> 00:48:55.920
a shark anyways. It’s not like you’re
going to be thriving down there. It’s not

00:48:55.920 --> 00:49:01.680
a big cavern like, you know, like the Pinocchio
whale; it’s solid and there’s no oxygen

00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:05.940
and so you’re dead. But that isn’t what
happens. Jonah’s in the whale and he’s

00:49:05.940 --> 00:49:09.920
kind of freaked out by this because now he’s
in a whale and there’s a storm and God’s

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:13.309
angry at him. So, the first thing he does is
pray and tell God what an idea he his and

00:49:13.309 --> 00:49:17.289
that, you know, he’d like to be forgiven.
And so the whale zips up to shore and vomits

00:49:17.289 --> 00:49:23.940
him onto the beach, which the very very undignified
mode of arriving anywhere.

00:49:23.940 --> 00:49:25.240
[Students’ laughter]

00:49:25.240 --> 00:49:30.829
So, that’s the Jonah story. And, then, of
course Jonah picks himself up and he thinks,

00:49:30.829 --> 00:49:34.490
“Yeah yeah, you know, that wasn’t so good.
It was a little rough. I think maybe I’m

00:49:34.490 --> 00:49:38.559
going off to Nineveh to tell the Ninehvites,
or whoever they were, that they’re just

00:49:38.559 --> 00:49:42.690
not living properly because that’s a lot
better than making God angry. You know, because

00:49:42.690 --> 00:49:46.400
what with the storms and the fisherman and
the whales and all that.” So, then, he goes

00:49:46.400 --> 00:49:50.130
off to Nineveh and, you know, he tells them
what’s going on and it seems to more or

00:49:50.130 --> 00:49:55.640
less work. So, that’s a very interesting
story and you think, “Well, how did people

00:49:55.640 --> 00:50:01.599
believe that?” I mean, and one question
might be: well, do you believe that story?

00:50:01.599 --> 00:50:06.119
And then you think, “Well no,” but then
you think, “Well maybe that’s more complex

00:50:06.119 --> 00:50:10.289
question than you might think because it turns
out that I believed the Pinocchio story.”

00:50:10.289 --> 00:50:13.770
And you say, “Well, no I didn’t really
believe it,” and then I would say, “Well

00:50:13.770 --> 00:50:19.089
it kind of depends on what you mean by believe,
doesn’t it?” Because you watched it and

00:50:19.089 --> 00:50:22.670
while you were watching it man you were into
it, you understood it, you didn’t want it

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:28.099
to be interrupted, you followed it. So, it’s
like, did you believe it? No, you were like

00:50:28.099 --> 00:50:32.440
a four-year-old skeptic sitting there thinking,
“Well, you know, puppet, whale. No, it doesn’t

00:50:32.440 --> 00:50:36.750
add up.” You’re not; you’re acting out
your belief in it by being engaged in it and

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:40.640
if someone taps you on the shoulder and wakes
you up thirty years later or twenty years

00:50:40.640 --> 00:50:44.920
later you say, “Oh no, I don’t believe
that.” It’s like, you know, it’s kind

00:50:44.920 --> 00:50:46.359
of remarkable what you’ll believe.

00:50:46.359 --> 00:50:52.079
I mean, how many of you are Star Wars like
aficionados? You know you won’t admit it,

00:50:52.079 --> 00:50:52.460
will you?

00:50:52.460 --> 00:50:52.910
[Students’ laughter]

00:50:52.910 --> 00:50:57.809
Yeah, but there’s lots of you in here.
I know. You know, and so that’s all mythology.

00:50:57.809 --> 00:51:02.569
It’s all mythology. You know that of course,
the person who What was his name? The guy

00:51:02.569 --> 00:51:07.900
who came up with the Stars Wars idea. Lucas.
Lucas was a student of Joseph Campbell. Joseph

00:51:07.900 --> 00:51:14.470
Campbell was a psychologist/mythologist and
the Star Wars stories are updated mythology

00:51:14.470 --> 00:51:18.309
just like Harry Potter and all the other things
that capture people’s imagination. So, you

00:51:18.309 --> 00:51:24.750
bloody well do believe this. And there’s
a reason for it too. Now what time is it?

00:51:24.750 --> 00:51:28.730
Ah, we’ll stop there. I’ll tell you the
reason the next time we meet.

00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:29.230
[Students’ laughter]

